WEBVTT

NOTE Narrated by a synthetic voice (an ElevenLabs stock voice); the opening disclosure is the macOS Trinoids voice. Text by Phil Renato.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.622
Eschatica,

00:00:02.622 --> 00:00:04.990
(banal mid-life asthmageddon)

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:11.560
Between February 7, 2009 and October 4, 2015 I updated my Facebook status

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:16.898
eighteen thousand, eight hundred ninety-six times. When factoring in a

00:00:16.898 --> 00:00:23.010
break from April 9 through November 22, 2014, that is well above eight updates a

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:29.045
day; or about one every two hours that I was likely awake. I uploaded more than

00:00:29.045 --> 00:00:34.615
one hundred videos, more than ten thousand photos, shared fifteen hundred

00:00:34.615 --> 00:00:38.870
sixty URLs and logged five thousand eighty-eight quotes.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:43.934
Do I want those hours back? I downloaded all of the data before deactivating my

00:00:43.934 --> 00:00:49.062
account, so I still have the work-product itself, though stripped of some of the

00:00:49.062 --> 00:00:53.931
context in the form of things from friends that I was responding to. Besides

00:00:53.931 --> 00:00:58.865
just thinking about it transactionally, was it good discipline — what was the

00:00:58.865 --> 00:01:03.864
effect of this media on my psyche, my teaching, my relationships and my art? I

00:01:03.864 --> 00:01:08.928
logged off entirely on October 4, I am writing this sentence in early November,

00:01:08.928 --> 00:01:14.056
and I’m still not sure about any of these questions. I miss reading some of what

00:01:14.056 --> 00:01:18.860
some people post, and I kind of miss being pissed off about the stupid shit

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:24.119
that the rest of them posted. I am still reading, still watching, still making and

00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:29.052
still listening to pretty much the same kinds of books and TV and objects and

00:01:29.052 --> 00:01:33.402
music I used to post about — but not sharing my favorites of each. I

00:01:33.402 --> 00:01:38.141
definitely have a little more time — and what time I have is a little less

00:01:38.141 --> 00:01:43.075
uninterrupted. Thinking about what I posted over those years, reviewing these

00:01:43.075 --> 00:01:47.879
posts, may be helpful in eventually answering the questions above and more.

00:01:47.879 --> 00:01:52.890
Skimming years worth of my own posts is the verbal equivalent of a part of your

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:57.322
life flashing before your eyes (the images don’t show up inline in the

00:01:57.322 --> 00:02:02.333
downloaded FB data.) It’s link, rant, quote, link, comment, quote, quote, rant.

00:02:02.333 --> 00:02:07.601
Not all of the quotes are from what I was hearing, watching and reading — some were

00:02:07.601 --> 00:02:12.740
compensating for the lack of italics in the Facebook markup language. But most do

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:17.751
seem, now that I’ve imported everything I’ve ever posted into a spreadsheet for

00:02:17.751 --> 00:02:22.890
examination, to be from the various media I consume. And most of them were posted

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:27.773
without citation or attribution, leaving me just a little better off than the

00:02:27.773 --> 00:02:32.783
seven hundred ninety-seven friends who may have occasionally seen these nuggets

00:02:32.783 --> 00:02:37.537
in their newsfeeds. As I scroll through them, I can remember the source and

00:02:37.537 --> 00:02:42.098
context for quite a few, and I’ve found six that are duplicates so far —

00:02:42.098 --> 00:02:47.045
sometimes years apart; four from the show 30Rock alone. And a lot were lyrics.

00:02:47.045 --> 00:02:54.383
Thursday, February 19, 2009 at 12:59pm EST,

00:02:54.383 --> 00:03:01.439
“You point your gun at someone else, but (you) always aim it for yourself!”.

00:03:01.439 --> 00:03:06.661
Endpoint’s After Taste was the first record I bought when I arrived in Ann

00:03:06.661 --> 00:03:12.240
Arbor in 1994. It is one of many albums that I’ve now listened to as many times

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:18.033
as Jane’s Addiction’s Nothing’s Shocking. It still strikes me that the combination

00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:23.040
of melody, metal and vocals fit surprisingly well together — and lyrics

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.691
like those above, from the song Lowland, have meant something worth remembering,

00:03:28.691 --> 00:03:34.556
though something different each time I’ve listened to it. “How many bridges must be

00:03:34.556 --> 00:03:35.200
burned?”.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:41.964
Sunday, April 19, 2009 at 8:59am EDT “Driving home, between the lines in the

00:03:41.964 --> 00:03:49.269
road. I swear that I’ve been through this before. Nothing makes much sense, except

00:03:49.269 --> 00:03:53.869
for doing yourself in...We all have what it takes…”.

00:03:53.869 --> 00:03:59.139
Stop reading this and search Google or your favorite streaming service for the

00:03:59.139 --> 00:04:04.682
Hot Water Music song Driving Home. Listen to it. I’m not sure any song but Cats in

00:04:04.682 --> 00:04:10.089
the Cradle or maybe Elton John’s Daniel has ever made me feel quite the way this

00:04:10.089 --> 00:04:10.773
song does.

00:04:10.773 --> 00:04:18.107
Sunday, July 19, 2009 at 3:45pm EDT,

00:04:18.107 --> 00:04:20.522
“Aloha, motherfuckers!”.

00:04:20.522 --> 00:04:25.675
Detroit was a mythical place, growing up in Southeast Michigan; filled with art,

00:04:25.675 --> 00:04:30.829
sports and arson. Coleman Alexander Young was elected mayor five months before I

00:04:30.829 --> 00:04:35.656
was born and was the object of more insults in my suburban community than I

00:04:35.656 --> 00:04:40.613
could possibly catalog. Given the overall level of education and crazy Reagan

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:45.441
Democrattery of my parents and their friends, the one thing I could believe

00:04:45.441 --> 00:04:50.790
about Young was that he had a foul mouth; and I loved it: “Swearing is an art form.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:55.291
You can express yourself much more exactly, much more succinctly, with

00:04:55.291 --> 00:04:57.117
properly used curse words.”.

00:04:57.117 --> 00:05:03.711
Thursday, August 6, 2009 at 9:26pm EDT,

00:05:03.711 --> 00:05:09.346
“Being in the Element, and especially being in the zone, doesn’t take energy

00:05:09.346 --> 00:05:11.696
away from you; it gives it to you.”.

00:05:11.696 --> 00:05:16.822
After I saw Ken Robinson’s TED talk for the first time, I read his book The

00:05:16.822 --> 00:05:22.294
Element. I wasn’t super impressed — but there’s some interesting stuff in there.

00:05:22.294 --> 00:05:27.419
It seemed poignant enough in 2009 for me to share it with my Facebook posse

00:05:27.419 --> 00:05:27.904
anyway.

00:05:27.904 --> 00:05:33.868
Saturday, July 24, 2010 at 10:13am EDT Having tea in the oldest museum

00:05:33.868 --> 00:05:40.265
restaurant in the world. Morris isn’t really my taste...Black tea is pretty

00:05:40.265 --> 00:05:47.181
good though. “There is nothing better for a man than that he should eat and drink

00:05:47.181 --> 00:05:50.984
and make his soul enjoy good in his labour.”.

00:05:50.984 --> 00:05:57.147
That is one of few Bible quotes I’ve ever typed out. I disliked it before I even

00:05:57.147 --> 00:06:03.466
found its derivation, Ecclesiastes 2:24. I love eating, and do too much of it, but

00:06:03.466 --> 00:06:06.820
don’t like thinking or talking about eating.

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:13.876
Tuesday, March 8, 2011 at 8:56pm EST,

00:06:13.876 --> 00:06:15.966
“Who is Justice Beaver?”.

00:06:15.966 --> 00:06:18.474
“He’s a crime fighting beaver.”.

00:06:18.474 --> 00:06:23.789
By 2011 NetFlix was my TV. The Office sucked up a few months of my life —

00:06:23.789 --> 00:06:29.620
first, the British version and then the oddly superior American version. Jim and

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:32.499
Dwight have an amazingly deep repartee.

00:06:32.499 --> 00:06:37.547
Facebook was my notebook — and as such, it wasn’t terribly important that anyone

00:06:37.547 --> 00:06:42.532
else be able to make sense of what I was taking note of. If I was doing this in

00:06:42.532 --> 00:06:47.580
the dark 90s I would have needed to cite every one in the entry itself, but in a

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:52.373
Google Earth it’s enough for me to freeze them, to be thawed at a time of my

00:06:52.373 --> 00:06:57.102
choosing. As I look back at these entries, I am vaguely familiar with about

00:06:57.102 --> 00:07:01.958
half of the uncited entries, clueless about a quarter of them, and relatively

00:07:01.958 --> 00:07:07.007
sure I remember the specific context for the remaining quarter. But Google helps

00:07:07.007 --> 00:07:11.991
me check each. For the next few, I’ll paste them in without checking them using

00:07:11.991 --> 00:07:16.784
Wikipedia et al and write a summary caption for the bunch that I won’t alter

00:07:16.784 --> 00:07:19.404
to fit the facts. You’ll have to trust me.

00:07:19.404 --> 00:07:25.998
Wednesday, March 16, 2011 at 9:52pm EDT,

00:07:25.998 --> 00:07:30.918
“Remember people: just don’t be yourselves.”.

00:07:30.918 --> 00:07:37.234
Sunday, July 3, 2011 at 12:16pm EDT,

00:07:37.234 --> 00:07:42.943
“That glittering may not be gold, don’t let nobody play me...”.

00:07:42.943 --> 00:07:50.649
Friday, February 10, 2012 at 2:04pm EST,

00:07:50.649 --> 00:07:56.826
“The truth must not be only the truth; it must also be told and acted upon.”.

00:07:56.826 --> 00:08:04.160
Monday, March 19, 2012 at 10:24pm EDT,

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:06.854
“Wanting to be book is not book.”.

00:08:06.854 --> 00:08:13.448
Saturday, April 21, 2012 at 10:53pm EDT,

00:08:13.448 --> 00:08:20.359
“Healer, gun enthusiast, jewelry designer; there is nothing this man can’t

00:08:20.359 --> 00:08:20.782
do.”.

00:08:20.782 --> 00:08:25.487
These words were written to be emphatically delivered in particular

00:08:25.487 --> 00:08:30.690
contexts and reading them as separated from the comedies, dramas, raps and

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:36.107
politics they’re a part of is a little jarring — especially in the aggregate.

00:08:36.107 --> 00:08:41.596
There are clues in the timestamps — the two in the early afternoon couldn’t be

00:08:41.596 --> 00:08:47.156
from NetFlix, I have a little more self control than that. “Remember people...”

00:08:47.156 --> 00:08:52.644
is funny, but also a little sad — it’s got to be one of Adrian Monk’s inverted

00:08:52.644 --> 00:08:58.133
clichés; I feel it in my temples. The “glittering may not be gold” is where my

00:08:58.133 --> 00:09:03.907
jaw meets my neck, I can feel Lupe Fiasco spitting it out. (Lupe’s lyric is one of

00:09:03.907 --> 00:09:09.110
the quotes that was repeated, in this case a total of three times over two

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:14.528
years.) “The truth” has Marxism written into it — I’m pretty sure it’s either

00:09:14.528 --> 00:09:20.159
Lenin, Che, or Mao — I’m able to recall the style somewhere in the upper rear of

00:09:20.159 --> 00:09:25.505
my skull, but I can’t think of what I would have been reading in February of

00:09:25.505 --> 00:09:31.279
2012 that would have contained it. “Book” I can see just behind my eyes, with Jack

00:09:31.279 --> 00:09:36.767
Donaghy, Jenna Maroney and Khonani (as Conan) in the hallway at 30Rock — maybe

00:09:36.767 --> 00:09:42.042
about to get on an an elevator. The last one I’m having trouble with — it’s

00:09:42.042 --> 00:09:47.459
definitely not from music, and it flows more like TV than any book I remember

00:09:47.459 --> 00:09:53.233
reading from that period. My instinct is that it’s from the show Portlandia, but I

00:09:53.233 --> 00:09:59.078
know I hadn’t seen that show in 2012 — so I have to guess: Vincent D’Onofrio in Law

00:09:59.078 --> 00:10:00.860
&amp; Order: Criminal Intent.

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:07.222
Thursday, June 7, 2012 at 11:09am EDT,

00:10:07.222 --> 00:10:11.909
“Even the hatred of squalor makes the brow grow stern.”.

00:10:11.909 --> 00:10:18.086
Monday, July 9, 2012 at 9:25pm EDT,

00:10:18.086 --> 00:10:23.281
“In Cambridge, you were not exactly taught: you just read books and talked

00:10:23.281 --> 00:10:24.074
about them.”.

00:10:24.074 --> 00:10:30.297
Friday, July 27, 2012 at 6:53pm EDT,

00:10:30.297 --> 00:10:34.198
“Much of what appears “natural” today dates from the 1980s”.

00:10:34.198 --> 00:10:40.421
Friday, September 6 2013 at 1:01pm EDT,

00:10:40.421 --> 00:10:46.037
“His self possession allowed him to be and act precisely as he was.”.

00:10:46.037 --> 00:10:52.399
Monday, April 7, 2014 at 8:14am EDT,

00:10:52.399 --> 00:10:58.062
“If you’re busy, you’re important. You’re leading a full and worthy life...”.

00:10:58.062 --> 00:11:05.214
Wednesday, December 24, 2014 at 1:58pm EST,

00:11:05.214 --> 00:11:10.970
“Heap up gold, and be eternalized for some wondrous cure.”.

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:17.518
Friday, January 2, 2015 at 4:13pm EST,

00:11:17.518 --> 00:11:23.609
“Concepts are densely stitched together through relationships of similarity and

00:11:23.609 --> 00:11:24.295
context.”.

00:11:24.295 --> 00:11:28.809
Even scrolling through more than five thousand quotes, they seem connected.

00:11:28.809 --> 00:11:33.689
Perhaps because I still have some context of them from having read the books they

00:11:33.689 --> 00:11:38.569
were extracted from — but also because I tend to collect things that are not only

00:11:38.569 --> 00:11:43.204
interesting to me, but which are related to another in some way. They are not

00:11:43.204 --> 00:11:47.962
necessarily coherent at first glance, they aren’t the products of the same mind

00:11:47.962 --> 00:11:52.476
or products of working on the same problems, but these ideas seem like they

00:11:52.476 --> 00:11:56.014
could fit together quite well. Here is a quick aggregation:

00:11:56.014 --> 00:12:01.676
Much of what appears natural to us today is from the 1980s, when concepts were

00:12:01.676 --> 00:12:07.044
densely stitched together through relationships of similarity and context.

00:12:07.044 --> 00:12:13.074
At Cambridge, we were not exactly taught: we just read books and talked about them.

00:12:13.074 --> 00:12:18.884
We read and discussed ‘heaping up gold to be eternalized for wondrous cures’ and

00:12:18.884 --> 00:12:24.840
assumed that our self possession allowed us to be and act precisely as we were. If

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:30.502
we we were busy, we were important. We were leading full and worthy lives, but

00:12:30.502 --> 00:12:34.326
even the hatred of squalor made our brows grow stern.

00:12:34.326 --> 00:12:37.995
Not a trainwreck, but not quite lyric philosophy either.

00:12:37.995 --> 00:12:43.362
Every once in a while, one of my Facebook friends would ask for attribution for one

00:12:43.362 --> 00:12:48.270
of the bare quotes I posted, and I was happy to provide the citation and the

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:53.179
link to the book, album, article, or podcast. These interactions rarely went

00:12:53.179 --> 00:12:57.957
more than a few comments deep — but occasionally would lead to interesting

00:12:57.957 --> 00:13:03.062
conversations on the topics of the quote or the quality of the author or artist

00:13:03.062 --> 00:13:08.036
who used them — and occasionally they would lead to fights. I tend not to get

00:13:08.036 --> 00:13:13.010
into super hot flamewars, because I can extinguish them with Facebook’s block

00:13:13.010 --> 00:13:17.984
button. I had seven hundred ninety-seven friends on Facebook, but had blocked

00:13:17.984 --> 00:13:23.286
seventy-eight others. I had unsubscribed from seeing six hundred thirty of them in

00:13:23.286 --> 00:13:28.456
my newsfeed. I was invited (en masse) to one thousand twenty events, and replied

00:13:28.456 --> 00:13:33.561
to only thirteen. I was ‘poked’ seven times, but never poked back. Because fuck

00:13:33.561 --> 00:13:34.281
picquerism.

00:13:34.281 --> 00:13:39.027
If we wanted to look up an article or book by keyword in libraries of the

00:13:39.027 --> 00:13:44.103
nineties we had to hope that someone happened to choose the exact same word to

00:13:44.103 --> 00:13:48.718
embed with the catalog entry. Standing there, at a skinned Windows 3.11

00:13:48.718 --> 00:13:53.991
terminal, we would type word after word into a slow search window until something

00:13:53.991 --> 00:13:59.133
popped up that seemed on point. Now we are used to full text search. Of course,

00:13:59.133 --> 00:14:04.077
this is messy — but it keeps the number of interactions with librarians at a

00:14:04.077 --> 00:14:09.416
minimum. I like the illusion that I am my own best filter and that the things that

00:14:09.416 --> 00:14:14.558
end up in my journal are the things that stood out; the best of everything that

00:14:14.558 --> 00:14:19.898
passed by my eyes and ears. So how can I query this dataset? I can count things, I

00:14:19.898 --> 00:14:22.996
can pick things out at random or in some rhythm.

00:14:22.996 --> 00:14:28.293
In the paragraph I aggregated from quotes, my eye/mind isolated out the

00:14:28.293 --> 00:14:34.196
following words as key: natural, concept, relate, context, read, talk, discuss,

00:14:34.196 --> 00:14:39.948
gold, eternal, wonder, cure, possess, precise, lead, worth, life, hate, made,

00:14:39.948 --> 00:14:45.699
grow. As I read them back to myself I realize that I have a hard time letting

00:14:45.699 --> 00:14:51.072
go of finding keys in almost every possible word. Which isn’t a terribly

00:14:51.072 --> 00:14:56.597
efficient search strategy, which might say a lot about my judgment. Having

00:14:56.597 --> 00:15:02.424
chosen these, I wonder what the frequency of each was in my Facebook data set?

00:15:02.424 --> 00:15:08.278
That’s a lot of reading and making, and little eternal context. Given what I know

00:15:08.278 --> 00:15:13.840
about my own behaviors, interests and social media practices — I’m not at all

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:19.694
surprised about the words I used a lot. And those two things are inclusive enough

00:15:19.694 --> 00:15:25.329
to have some of the low frequency words inside their envelope. And, of course,

00:15:25.329 --> 00:15:30.817
there are synonyms for many that may help to bolster counts of the essential

00:15:30.817 --> 00:15:36.233
meaning of some words within the set. But let’s assume these are reasonably

00:15:36.233 --> 00:15:41.941
representative of my levels of interest in these ideas, despite being counts of

00:15:41.941 --> 00:15:47.648
keywords from quotes I randomly plucked from one source (which itself contained

00:15:47.648 --> 00:15:53.283
quotes from the plurality of my sources.) Of the nineteen words, only one is a

00:15:53.283 --> 00:15:58.991
material noun, gold. Grow, hate, lead, possess, wonder, talk, discuss, read and

00:15:58.991 --> 00:16:04.333
relate are verbs. Natural, eternal, worth, precise and made are adjectives

00:16:04.333 --> 00:16:09.383
and concept, context and life are ~immaterial nouns. The concretes are

00:16:09.383 --> 00:16:15.017
about 8% of the results, the actioning about 46%, the attributal are about 36%

00:16:15.017 --> 00:16:20.872
and the abstractions are about 10% of the frequency counts, even though they make

00:16:20.872 --> 00:16:26.653
up 11%, 47%, 26% and 16% of the relative numbers of words in this list, as split

00:16:26.653 --> 00:16:32.141
up into word types. Searching for the numerological could go deeper: summing

00:16:32.141 --> 00:16:37.922
the letters gives us 8, 46, 30 and 18 per category; the pattern keeps repeating.

00:16:37.922 --> 00:16:43.630
That distribution demonstrates either phrase collection or phrase analysis that

00:16:43.630 --> 00:16:49.338
is not hysterically attracted to action or description balanced with ideas that

00:16:49.338 --> 00:16:55.119
are more general than specific — there is a balanced asymmetry pairing each data

00:16:55.119 --> 00:16:55.412
set.

00:16:55.412 --> 00:17:00.264
This diversity seems, without going through this exercise ad nauseam, to

00:17:00.264 --> 00:17:05.048
permeate the posts I made in general — regularly returning to themes of

00:17:05.048 --> 00:17:10.447
identity, learning, losing, existence, non sequiturs and material culture. There

00:17:10.447 --> 00:17:16.051
is some seeming randomness, but also some structure. There are some similarities of

00:17:16.051 --> 00:17:21.314
style where there are dissimilarities of meaning, and some observations in the

00:17:21.314 --> 00:17:26.166
same stream that are jarringly, aesthetically incongruous. I think I can

00:17:26.166 --> 00:17:31.155
own that. And I think the habit is relatively gestalt; the pattern repeats

00:17:31.155 --> 00:17:35.871
as it jumps to the photographs, conversations, books and things I drew

00:17:35.871 --> 00:17:41.270
that didn’t make it to Facebook — mixes of the specific, the general, the active

00:17:41.270 --> 00:17:46.327
and the textured. The red, the speedy, the sharp and the symbolic; churning

00:17:46.327 --> 00:17:51.316
against one another in the maelstrom, pulling in comrades and spitting out

00:17:51.316 --> 00:17:52.068
remainders.

00:17:52.068 --> 00:17:57.820
Thursday, September 3, 2015 at 10:03am EST “Next to the domestication of fire,

00:17:57.820 --> 00:18:02.750
plants, animals, and of humans themselves, the invention of writing

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:07.979
constitutes probably the greatest technological transformation in human

00:18:07.979 --> 00:18:14.030
history (unless we count upright posture or language as technological innovations,

00:18:14.030 --> 00:18:19.932
which we have reason to do.) Like fire, writing has terrible powers. Its ability

00:18:19.932 --> 00:18:24.937
to preserve meaning makes it explosive and dangerous. Like all other

00:18:24.937 --> 00:18:30.390
domesticates, writing has never been fully tamed, and can occasionally run

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:36.217
amuck… Writing, as a medium, that allows voice and mind to transcend the grave,

00:18:36.217 --> 00:18:41.595
has a long-standing association with death and death-dealing men.” — John

00:18:41.595 --> 00:18:42.641
Dunham Peters.

00:18:42.641 --> 00:18:47.663
We can believe in Frankenstein so long as he isn’t in high definition. Models

00:18:47.663 --> 00:18:52.884
should be better than the world; but the more detail we put into them the faster

00:18:52.884 --> 00:18:58.170
they fall into uncanny valleys. It wasn’t just that Doc Brown didn’t have time to

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:03.523
build his model to scale or to paint it, it’s that micro Hill Valley was better in

00:19:03.523 --> 00:19:08.479
gesso covered boxes and bottles. Crude models can be ugly, but they assume a

00:19:08.479 --> 00:19:13.897
flexibility of purpose, function and form that the near-perfect will always fail to

00:19:13.897 --> 00:19:14.360
assume.

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:19.609
There is a link between introversion and nearsightedness, and it’s not just the

00:19:19.609 --> 00:19:24.723
metaphoric myopia. The extrovert is the noise of the worlds they hold inside,

00:19:24.723 --> 00:19:30.173
shouting down from great distances, with the confidence of seeing hemispheres at a

00:19:30.173 --> 00:19:35.489
time. The introvert is in a world made mute in insulated apertures; close enough

00:19:35.489 --> 00:19:40.805
to read the serifs, workshopping texts in conferences of one. The invaginate see

00:19:40.805 --> 00:19:46.054
that analog order breaks stochastically at some proximity, that the visible can

00:19:46.054 --> 00:19:51.437
be split when filtered for one variable at a time and that landscapes are made in

00:19:51.437 --> 00:19:55.273
minds (looking to compress even the smallest infinities.).

00:19:55.273 --> 00:20:00.756
Choices fade as the switch is thrown. The power is in potential. Our energy, once

00:20:00.756 --> 00:20:05.691
released, can’t be kept from its consequences — electrons follow the path

00:20:05.691 --> 00:20:10.900
they were put on. The resources and strategies we store up are magic in their

00:20:10.900 --> 00:20:15.492
batteries — cells filled with differential energy, awaiting a closed

00:20:15.492 --> 00:20:20.838
circuit to unleash them on one another. Choices about leadership are calculated

00:20:20.838 --> 00:20:25.978
in competency, in style, in procedure — but they are actually about vectors.

00:20:25.978 --> 00:20:31.324
There are niggles to be armwrestled over, but they don’t change the probability

00:20:31.324 --> 00:20:35.025
curve effects of a magnitude in a particular direction.
